### Autopilot-full

**You own the verdicts, never the PRs. One owner runs each PR build to merge; nothing merges without your clean swarm verdict.** For "autopilot this queue", "full autopilot", one-owner-per-PR programs. [Orchestrate](orchestrate.md) runs a standing program: coordinator lands verified work itself, workers never merge. Here each PR's owner carries the whole lifecycle through merge; root keeps only verification, countersigns, audits.

1. **Mark operator's items, honor state-then-wait.** Operator-named items stay with the operator: they review and click, no owner merges one. Operator asks for the protocol or plan stated? Deliver, stop. Execution starts only on explicit go. On that go, create a goal with the full program objective. Goal continues across turns until the queue is done.
2. **Spawn one owner per PR with the full lifecycle and an early trail.** Resolve the forge once. Use GitHub CLI (`gh`) for PR create, edit, view, watch, merge. Never require Graphite (`gt`). One background subagent per PR owns build, first push, ready PR, self-proof on the real artifact (the [prove it works](../../prove-it-works/SKILL.md) principle skill), a slop-strip ([unslop](../../unslop/SKILL.md)), [`/no-comments`](../../no-comments/SKILL.md) (the [no comments](../../no-comments/SKILL.md) skill), a rebase onto current trunk, and the merge. Within ~15 minutes, every owner starts a `decisions.tsv` trail per the [show me your work](../../show-me-your-work/SKILL.md) skill, pushes its first branch snapshot, opens the PR ready (never draft). Open the PR before self-proof so URL, decisions, checks form a durable trail. Keep `decisions.tsv` uncommitted, return it with reports. Subagent starts? Owner adds its ID, expected runtime (at least the longest past run of that kind), state to a `children.tsv` kept the same way. Owner does the first rebase before the code-ready report, whether or not trunk drifted. Fix rounds: owner keeps that merge base. Owner rebases again only at merge prep (step 5), on a `git merge-tree` conflict with trunk, or on a CI failure from a trunk change. Shipped code final, after slop-strip and [`/no-comments`](../../no-comments/SKILL.md): reports the code-ready head SHA. Also reports the SHA of each later push that changes the patch. Self-proof and CI then run parallel with the [swarm](../../swarm/SKILL.md). Owner reports merge-ready with the head SHA when self-proof and CI finish. Before a push that starts a round, run the pre-review checks the repo's AGENTS.md files and rules name for the touched paths. Run them on the committed head. A hook pass is not proof. Publish each rebase: push the owner's own branch with `git push --force-with-lease` after an `ls-remote` check. Never force-push a shared branch. The merge is the one step an owner may not take alone. Step 4 gates it.
3. **Run owners in true parallel, never stack.** Many owners at once when PRs are self-contained: one writer per branch, disjoint files, cross-PR drift absorbed by rebase. Genuinely overlapping work serializes. Self-contained PRs branch straight off main; sequenced work is merge-then-branch. One exception: an owner splitting a genuinely dependent change may hold a short private base-branch stack.
4. **Swarm-verify every round before its merge.** A round starts at the owner's code-ready head SHA and at each later push that changes the patch. At that SHA, fan out parallel independent verifiers per the [swarm](../../swarm/SKILL.md) skill, aggregate to one verdict. The merge needs a clean verdict from the round whose patch matches the merge-ready head. Audit the receipts in the merge-ready report before the verdict. Lanes: re-run the gates at that SHA. Prove the load-bearing behavior live on the real surface (through the [drive surface](../../drive-surface/SKILL.md) skill, or a named driver where none exists). Audit the diff, distrusting the PR body. Run the audit as two or more review lanes with the full brief. One main focus per lane, e.g. consumer parity with trunk, lifetimes and races, data and config safety. **Regression lane against trunk.** Same load-bearing scenario on current trunk. Trunk lacks the feature? Record that, gate the behavior the diff adds plus the end state the user waits for, don't pretend trunk can produce it. The live lane is the floor; a verdict without it is not clean. No merge without the root's clean verdict. Lanes return? Send every proven finding against the PR to the owner in one fix-forward. A defect a lane filed as a note is a finding. Each behavior finding: ask for a red test covering every site with the same defect. No test can show it? Ask for a repro receipt. Add that defect to the next round's review brief. New head gets a fresh swarm and verdict, except lane results still valid under the patch-id rule in [`playbooks/shipping.md`](shipping.md).
5. **On a clean verdict the owner merges, takes the next item.** Owner merges only from a head freshly rebased onto trunk. Merge prep never before a round's lanes start; ends with a rebase onto current trunk right before the merge. After the merge-prep rebase, owner reports the new head SHA. CI must pass on that head before merge; the patch-id rule decides whether the round's verdict holds. Trunk moves again before merge? The patch-id rule in [`playbooks/shipping.md`](shipping.md) governs re-verification. New head voids the verdict unless the patch-id is unchanged. Owner squash-merges its own PR, picks up its next self-contained item. The operator's full-autonomy grant plus the root's clean verdict is the merge authorization; a green check alone never is. Operator-named items stop at merge-ready, wait for the click.
6. **Run the root layer.** A genuinely new raise of a pinned gate or budget value (a limit CI only lets tighten) needs your fresh countersign, only after verifier proof. Operator's grant or standing orders cover approvals? That countersign is the approval. Owner records it in the form the tool's approval contract allows, with a pointer to the root's countersign. A lane checks the record against that countersign. Root never gives or bypasses an approval the forge enforces. Absorbing values already landed on main is drift, not a raise. Audit tick over all owners roughly every 30 minutes. A local root arms each tick as a background loop with a monitored 30-minute sleep that emits an output-notification sentinel. Never leave the cadence to memory or lossy completion notifications. Each tick: re-read this playbook from trunk, re-read the armed goal, audit against both, fix drift. Probe each owner with a generic liveness or status check, collect the decision trails. Count only side effects as progress: commits, pushes, PR or check deltas, store reports. Lane errors, or passes expected runtime with no side effect? Stuck. Stand it down, dispatch a replacement at once. Don't wait for a polite return. Each tick runs the lane stuck test over the program's agent list, where the platform has one, and over every owner's `children.tsv`. Stop works or not: the root has the owner record each stuck subagent as stuck, and replace it if its work is still needed. Each replacement that stalls gets the same steps. The root takes both steps when the owner cannot. A stall never proves or drops the work. Merges batch? Run a retro pass. End the tick only when no delegated work is left, even after the last merge.
7. **Stand down instantly on the operator's stop.** Operator's hold or stand-down reaches every owner as a zero-writes order immediately. Owners hold their briefs until the operator releases them.

**Reply:** the queue with each PR's owner, state, head SHA. Each verdict and the swarm that produced it. What merged, what each owner took next. Countersigns granted and why. Open operator gates. Where the collected decision trails live.
